From: Kali NetHunter rhodep port
Subject: [PATCH] nfc: route the MIFARE listen to the embedded secure element

Patch 0112 enumerates the eSE (NFCEE id 0x83 on rhodep, the one whose supported
protocol is MIFARE), which is where an Android transit app provisions a MIFARE
card. The applet persists across the OS change and the eSE runs Crypto1 itself,
so to make the card answer a reader from Linux the controller only has to enable
that NFCEE and route the MIFARE listen to it.

Remember the eSE's NFCEE id when it turns up in an NFCEE_DISCOVER_NTF (the one
that lists the MIFARE protocol). When card emulation is requested and an eSE was
found, NFCEE_MODE_SET(ENABLE) it and route the MIFARE protocol entry in the
listen mode routing table to that NFCEE instead of the host.

When steering to the eSE, three things differ from host emulation:

  - Route the NFC-A *technology* to the eSE, not just the MIFARE protocol.
    MIFARE Classic selection happens at the NFC-A technology level, so the eSE
    has to own the technology to present the card's own ATQA/SAK/UID.
  - Do NOT set any LA_* config (LA_NFCID1, LA_SEL_INFO, ...) from the host. The
    eSE owns the NFC-A identity; a host LA_NFCID1 would override the real card's
    UID and a host LA_SEL_INFO=0x08 would advertise ISO-DEP instead of MIFARE,
    so the reader would never see the provisioned card.
  - This controller does not light its listen front-end for NFC-A alone; it
    only wakes when NFC-F listen is also offered. But NFC-F with the firmware
    defaults arms an autonomous NFC-DEP (P2P) peer / Type-3 tag that answers the
    reader instead of routing the field to the eSE. Neutralise it in the
    emulation path -- LF_PROTOCOL_TYPE = 0 (no NFC-DEP over NFC-F) and
    LF_T3T_FLAGS = 0 (no Type-3 listen slots) -- and then offer NFC-A + NFC-F
    listen. NFC-F now only powers the analog front-end; the NFC-A field reaches
    the eSE.

The controller accepts all of this with status 0x0, but the card still does not
answer a reader (tested with the SUBE app and a Flipper Zero -- see docs/nfc.md).
An ETSI HCI network bring-up for the eSE was tried and does not apply: the eSE
NFCEE advertises the MIFARE protocol, not the HCI Access interface, so
CORE_CONN_CREATE(HCI_ACCESS) is rejected, and MIFARE card emulation does not use
the HCI/APDU path anyway. The remaining block is the NFC-A listen front-end not
engaging at RF, most likely a vendor RF profile the plain NCI path cannot
reproduce -- the same ceiling as host emulation.

--- a/include/net/nfc/nci.h	2026-09-01 22:59:53.266251007 +0000
+++ b/include/net/nfc/nci.h	2026-09-01 22:57:03.430301012 +0000
@@ -132,6 +132,7 @@
 #define NCI_LA_NFCID1						0x33
 #define NCI_LA_SEL_INFO						0x32
 #define NCI_LF_PROTOCOL_TYPE					0x50
+#define NCI_LF_T3T_FLAGS					0x3f
 #define NCI_LF_CON_BITR_F					0x54
 
 /* NCI Configuration Parameters masks */
--- a/include/net/nfc/nci_core.h	2026-09-01 22:59:46.862251004 +0000
+++ b/include/net/nfc/nci_core.h	2026-09-01 22:57:03.430301012 +0000
@@ -254,6 +254,16 @@
 	__u8			manufact_id;
 	__u32			manufact_specific_info;
 
+	/* NFCEE id of the embedded secure element (the one that lists the
+	 * MIFARE protocol), learned from NFCEE_DISCOVER_NTF. 0 = none found.
+	 * A provisioned MIFARE card lives here; routing the MIFARE listen to
+	 * this NFCEE lets it answer a reader. */
+	__u8			ese_nfcee_id;
+	/* NFCEE status byte from NFCEE_DISCOVER_NTF for the eSE (0x01 =
+	 * CONNECTED/ENABLED). Used to skip a redundant NFCEE_MODE_SET(ENABLE)
+	 * that the S3FWRN5 answers with status 0x03 (REJECTED). */
+	__u8			ese_enabled;
+
 	/* Save RF Discovery ID or NFCEE ID under conn_create */
 	struct dest_spec_params cur_params;
 	/* Save destination type under conn_create */
--- a/net/nfc/nci/ntf.c	2026-09-01 22:59:53.267251007 +0000
+++ b/net/nfc/nci/ntf.c	2026-09-01 22:57:03.430301012 +0000
@@ -958,6 +958,26 @@
 
 	nfcee_ntf = (struct nci_nfcee_discover_ntf *)skb->data;
 
+	/* The NCI 2.0 NFCEE_DISCOVER_NTF is variable length: id, status,
+	 * num_protocols, protocols[], num_tlvs, tlvs[]. Scan the supported
+	 * protocols for MIFARE (0x80); the NFCEE that lists it is the embedded
+	 * secure element holding a provisioned MIFARE card. Remember its id so
+	 * the emulation path can route the MIFARE listen to it. */
+	if (skb->len >= 3) {
+		u8 nfcee_id = skb->data[0];
+		u8 num_proto = skb->data[2];
+		unsigned int i;
+
+		for (i = 0; i < num_proto && 3 + i < skb->len; i++) {
+			if (skb->data[3 + i] == NCI_RF_PROTOCOL_MIFARE) {
+				ndev->ese_nfcee_id = nfcee_id;
+				/* Byte 1 is the NFCEE status (0x01 = ENABLED). */
+				ndev->ese_enabled = skb->data[1];
+				break;
+			}
+		}
+	}
+
 	/* NFCForum NCI 9.2.1 HCI Network Specific Handling
 	 * If the NFCC supports the HCI Network, it SHALL return one,
 	 * and only one, NFCEE_DISCOVER_NTF with a Protocol type of
--- a/net/nfc/nci/core.c	2026-09-02 00:58:13.502372006 +0000
+++ b/net/nfc/nci/core.c	2026-09-01 22:58:08.755575000 +0000
@@ -244,6 +244,26 @@
 	nci_send_cmd(ndev, NCI_OP_CORE_SET_CONFIG_CMD, (3 + param->len), &cmd);
 }
 
+/* Isolation switch for card emulation: when set, ignore any embedded secure
+ * element learned from NFCEE_DISCOVER_NTF and force the HOST listen path
+ * (LA_* NFC-A identity + LMRT to the host 0x00 + T2T/FRAME listen map). This
+ * lets a single build test host listen (does the RF front-end arm at all?)
+ * versus eSE routing, without reflashing. Use:  modprobe nci emulate_host=1
+ * or, at runtime:  echo 1 > /sys/module/nci/parameters/emulate_host  */
+static bool emulate_host;
+module_param(emulate_host, bool, 0644);
+MODULE_PARM_DESC(emulate_host,
+		 "force host card-emulation path, ignore the eSE (default 0)");
+
+/* Effective eSE NFCEE id for the emulation path: 0 (host path) when the
+ * emulate_host isolation switch is on, otherwise the id learned from
+ * NFCEE_DISCOVER_NTF. Both nci_start_poll() and the listen mode routing
+ * builder use this so the two stay consistent. */
+static inline u8 nci_emu_ese_id(const struct nci_dev *ndev)
+{
+	return emulate_host ? 0 : ndev->ese_nfcee_id;
+}
+
 struct nci_rf_discover_param {
 	__u32	im_protocols;
 	__u32	tm_protocols;
@@ -301,12 +301,13 @@
 			NCI_NFC_A_PASSIVE_LISTEN_MODE;
 		cmd.disc_configs[cmd.num_disc_configs].frequency = 1;
 		cmd.num_disc_configs++;
-		/* EXP-2: offer NFC-A and NFC-F listen together, even when
-		 * emulating. NFC-F listen is the one path that does activate on
-		 * this controller, and offering NFC-A alone never arms the A
-		 * listener -- the mainline core also never offers A listen on
-		 * its own. Pairing them may be what makes the firmware arm the
-		 * A listener. */
+		/* Offer NFC-F listen too. This controller does not light its
+		 * listen front-end for NFC-A alone -- it only wakes when NFC-F
+		 * listen is also offered. The NFC-DEP/Type-3 peer that NFC-F
+		 * would normally present has been neutralised in the emulation
+		 * path (LF_PROTOCOL_TYPE = 0, LF_T3T_FLAGS = 0), so offering it
+		 * here only powers the analog front-end; the NFC-A field then
+		 * reaches the host or the eSE via the routing table. */
 		cmd.disc_configs[cmd.num_disc_configs].rf_tech_and_mode =
 			NCI_NFC_F_PASSIVE_LISTEN_MODE;
 		cmd.disc_configs[cmd.num_disc_configs].frequency = 1;
@@ -873,12 +874,18 @@
 	cmd.more = 0;
 	cmd.num_entries = 0;
 
-	/* Route NFC-A technology to the host so the controller answers the
-	 * anticollision itself and comes up as an NFC-A tag. */
+	/* Route NFC-A technology. If a MIFARE-capable secure element was found,
+	 * route it there so the eSE handles the whole NFC-A layer -- the
+	 * anticollision with its own ATQA/SAK/UID from the provisioned card.
+	 * MIFARE Classic selection happens at the NFC-A technology level (it is
+	 * not an ISO-DEP protocol), so routing the technology to the eSE, not
+	 * just the MIFARE protocol, is what lets the eSE present the real card.
+	 * Without an eSE, route to the host so the host can be a bare NFC-A
+	 * tag. */
 	tech = (struct nci_lmrt_tech_entry *)p;
 	tech->type = NCI_LMRT_TYPE_TECHNOLOGY;
 	tech->len = 3;
-	tech->nfcee_id = NCI_NFCEE_ID_HOST;
+	tech->nfcee_id = nci_emu_ese_id(ndev) ? : NCI_NFCEE_ID_HOST;
 	tech->power_state = NCI_LMRT_POWER_STATE_ALL;
 	tech->technology = NCI_NFC_RF_TECHNOLOGY_A;
 	p += sizeof(*tech);
@@ -894,14 +901,16 @@
 	p += sizeof(*proto);
 	cmd.num_entries++;
 
-	/* Route the MIFARE Classic protocol (Samsung/NXP proprietary 0x80) to
-	 * the host as well. A MIFARE Classic card (SAK 0x08) is presented under
-	 * this protocol, not plain T2T, so without this entry the controller
-	 * accepts the NFC-A listen config but never activates for MIFARE. */
+	/* Route the MIFARE Classic protocol (Samsung/NXP proprietary 0x80).
+	 * A MIFARE Classic card is presented under this protocol, not plain
+	 * T2T. If an embedded secure element that supports MIFARE was found
+	 * (ese_nfcee_id), route it there: the eSE holds a provisioned MIFARE
+	 * card and answers a reader autonomously, running Crypto1 itself.
+	 * Otherwise fall back to the host. */
 	proto = (struct nci_lmrt_proto_entry *)p;
 	proto->type = NCI_LMRT_TYPE_PROTOCOL;
 	proto->len = 3;
-	proto->nfcee_id = NCI_NFCEE_ID_HOST;
+	proto->nfcee_id = nci_emu_ese_id(ndev) ? : NCI_NFCEE_ID_HOST;
 	proto->power_state = NCI_LMRT_POWER_STATE_ALL;
 	proto->protocol = NCI_RF_PROTOCOL_MIFARE;
 	p += sizeof(*proto);
@@ -1024,34 +1024,72 @@
 		 * here corrupts the high ATQA byte). */
 		__u8 dur[2];
 
-		v = 0x04;
-		nci_set_config(ndev, NCI_LA_BIT_FRAME_SDD, 1, &v);
-		v = 0x00;
-		nci_set_config(ndev, NCI_LA_PLATFORM_CONFIG, 1, &v);
-		nci_set_config(ndev, NCI_LA_NFCID1,
-			       nfc_dev->emu_nfcid1_len, nfc_dev->emu_nfcid1);
-		v = nfc_dev->emu_sel_res;
-		nci_set_config(ndev, NCI_LA_SEL_INFO, 1, &v);
+		/* Host NFC-A identity (LA_*) ONLY when the host is the tag. When
+		 * a MIFARE card in the secure element is being presented, the eSE
+		 * owns the NFC-A identity -- its own ATQA/SAK/UID from the
+		 * provisioned card. Setting LA_NFCID1/LA_SEL_INFO from the host
+		 * here would override that with a wrong UID and a SAK that
+		 * advertises ISO-DEP (0x08) instead of MIFARE, so the reader
+		 * would never see the real card. Skip all LA_* for the eSE. */
+		if (!nci_emu_ese_id(ndev)) {
+			v = 0x04;
+			nci_set_config(ndev, NCI_LA_BIT_FRAME_SDD, 1, &v);
+			v = 0x00;
+			nci_set_config(ndev, NCI_LA_PLATFORM_CONFIG, 1, &v);
+			nci_set_config(ndev, NCI_LA_NFCID1,
+				       nfc_dev->emu_nfcid1_len,
+				       nfc_dev->emu_nfcid1);
+			v = nfc_dev->emu_sel_res;
+			nci_set_config(ndev, NCI_LA_SEL_INFO, 1, &v);
+		}
 
-		/* EXP-3: give discovery a real total duration (1000 ms, LE) so
-		 * the controller dwells in the NFC-A listen window long enough
-		 * to coincide with a reader's field. */
+		/* Give discovery a real total duration (1000 ms, LE) so the
+		 * controller dwells in the NFC-A listen window long enough to
+		 * coincide with a reader's field. */
 		dur[0] = 0xe8;
 		dur[1] = 0x03;
 		nci_set_config(ndev, NCI_TOTAL_DURATION, 2, dur);
 
+		/* This controller does not light its listen front-end for NFC-A
+		 * alone; it only comes up when NFC-F listen is also offered. But
+		 * offering NFC-F with the firmware defaults makes it arm an
+		 * autonomous NFC-DEP (P2P) peer / a Type-3 tag that answers the
+		 * reader instead of routing the field to us. LF_PROTOCOL_TYPE=0
+		 * alone suppresses the NFC-DEP peer. Do NOT also send
+		 * LF_T3T_FLAGS=0: with no matching LF_T3T_IDENTIFIERS the
+		 * S3FWRN5 rejects it (SET_CONFIG status 0x9) and that leaves the
+		 * listen config inconsistent, blocking activation. */
+		if (nci_emu_ese_id(ndev)) {
+			v = 0x00;
+			nci_set_config(ndev, NCI_LF_PROTOCOL_TYPE, 1, &v);
+		}
+
 		/* Canonical NCI listen order is
 		 * CORE_SET_CONFIG -> RF_DISCOVER_MAP -> LMRT -> RF_DISCOVER.
 		 * Send an RF_DISCOVER_MAP (T2T/FRAME/LISTEN) here, in the listen
 		 * window: the map done at open_device is not honoured for this
 		 * later RF_DISCOVER, and without a listen map the controller has
 		 * no interface to activate a tag with and never raises
 		 * RF_INTF_ACTIVATED_NTF. */
 		nci_request(ndev, nci_rf_discover_map_listen_req, NULL,
 			    msecs_to_jiffies(NCI_RF_DISC_TIMEOUT));
 
+		/* If the embedded secure element that holds a provisioned MIFARE
+		 * card was found, enable it so it can answer over RF -- but ONLY
+		 * if it is not already enabled. The eSE (0x83) enumerates with
+		 * NFCEE_DISCOVER_NTF status 0x01 (CONNECTED/ENABLED) and the
+		 * S3FWRN5 answers a redundant NFCEE_MODE_SET(ENABLE) on an
+		 * already-enabled NFCEE with status 0x03 (REJECTED). Skip it when
+		 * ese_enabled == 0x01; the MIFARE listen is routed to it in the
+		 * routing table below either way. This runs after RF_DISCOVER_MAP
+		 * and before the LMRT, per the canonical NCI order. */
+		if (nci_emu_ese_id(ndev) &&
+		    ndev->ese_enabled != NCI_NFCEE_ENABLE)
+			nci_nfcee_mode_set(ndev, nci_emu_ese_id(ndev),
+					   NCI_NFCEE_ENABLE);
+
 		/* Program the listen mode routing table so NFC-A / T2T are
 		 * routed to the host; without this the controller accepts the
 		 * NFC-A listen config but never activates as a tag. */
 		nci_request(ndev, nci_rf_set_listen_mode_routing_req, NULL,
 			    msecs_to_jiffies(NCI_RF_DISC_TIMEOUT));
